Z||000003||billyboy||03-05-2001||12:18 AM||||If you don't mind me asking, how did you add the weight of that V8 without having the back end sag?  The car looks sweet, I love that gold color... makes it look really classy, and you've definitely retained the sleeper look!<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||03-05-2001||12:45 AM||||That's the first gold 87 I've ever seen that wasn't a GT. Nice job!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||14
Z||000005||Richard Parnell||03-05-2001||12:52 AM||||Believe or not the suspension is still stock.  I've done nothing else except put all new rotors and calipers on it so I can stop.  I'm planning on upgrading the suspension with new springs, shocks, struts,bushings etc.  The rear doesn't sag because the car only weighs about 150 lbs more. About the same as having a passemger in the car. It does bottom out on big bumps at higher speeds though.<BR>All this is possible through the efforts of V8 Archie's research and developement of this conversion.||||reg||1
Z||000006||billyboy||03-05-2001||01:22 AM||||Hmmm... It may be worth getting a Fiero just to put a big 'ol 350 in it!  I bet you needed a helluva shoe horn to get that puppy in there, huh?  Never the less, your install looks completely sweet, I hope I can afford to do that some day.<P>Think an old Grand National T-Type or GNX motor would fit in there relatively easy?  I know you guys have already done the 3800 SC additions... didn't know if the turbocharged GN would fit or not... or if it would be sacrelige either...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||03-05-2001||09:44 AM||||Billyboy,<BR>I believe that Archie experimented with the GN/GNX swap, although no one currently offers a kit. Seems like it would have had all the complexity of a 3800 + 3.4 + V-8 swap. Required a trans adapter plate, plus starter relocation (I think), plus rewiring and fabrication of mounts.<BR>BTW... Welcome to the Forum. <P> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000008||Archie||03-05-2001||11:58 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Billyboy,<BR>I believe that Archie experimented with the GN/GNX swap, although no one currently offers a kit. Seems like it would have had all the complexity of a 3800 + 3.4 + V-8 swap. Required a trans adapter plate, plus starter relocation (I think), plus rewiring and fabrication of mounts.<BR>BTW... Welcome to the Forum. <P> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's right..... We do still have the drawings etc. to make more of them. We don't inventory them because of very low demand, so you'd be looking at 5 to 7 weeks delivery time. <P>This is not a full blown kit, it would be the adapter plate and starter mounting assembly. You'll make or buy the rest of the parts you'll need.<P>BTW, The car is lookin' good, Richard.<P>Archie<p>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 03-05-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||billyboy||03-05-2001||09:48 PM||||Thanks Raydar... unfortunately I don't really "own" a Fiero yet, but I'm trying ever so hard to find an 88 Formula, preferrably in yellow.  I think they made that color, didn't they?  If they didn't, then someone around here painted his that color and put the Formula stickers back on.  If I do find a nice Formula, I'll probably keep it stock.  If I can find an excellent 87-88 GT, I'll do a swap.  Reason I asked about the GNX is because a good friend has 2 T-Type motors and a GNX motor sitting in his garage from totalled Grand Nationals that he bought from insurance companies.  I don't think I'd do that to a Formula, since I'm finding that they're very hard to find, especially in good condition.<P>Anyway, thanks for the welcome to the club!  It's good to be here!<P>||||reg||1
